          "type": "text",
          "text": "`clamp(value, low, high)` now raises `ValueError` when `low > high`, and the standard test command catches it if the check is ever removed.\n\n**Changes:**\n- **`bounds.py`**: `clamp` checks the bounds first and raises `ValueError` with a message showing both values. When the bounds are valid it works exactly as before.\n- **`test_bounds.py`**: two new tests:\n  - `test_low_greater_than_high_raises` checks for `ValueError` with the value below, between and above the bounds.\n  - `test_equal_bounds` confirms that `low == high` is still allowed, so the fix can't be \"tightened\" to `>=` by mistake.\n\n**Verification:**\n- `python3 -m unittest discover -v` runs 3 tests and all pass.\n- I temporarily put back the old `bounds.py` and ran the tests again: 3 failures, all from the new error test (one per value it checks). So reintroducing the bug would fail the standard check. I then restored the fix."
